# M5Stack Cardputer-style external keyboard (Espressif vendor ID 303a — seen as
# product 1001 "StampS3 Keyboard" and product 8000 "M5-Cardputer Keyboard"
# across different firmware builds the user tested; matched by vendor only so
# this survives future firmware/product-ID changes).
#
# This keyboard's arrow-key cluster is Fn + , . ; / — but its firmware sends
# the literal comma/dot/semicolon/slash HID usage code whether or not Fn is
# held (confirmed with evtest across two different firmware builds: no
# distinguishable event, and no separate Fn keycode, differed between the
# plain and Fn-held cases for three of the four keys). It never emits a real
# HID arrow-key usage code (0x4F-0x52). There is no way to recover "Fn or not"
# at this layer since the firmware itself doesn't expose it — so this remaps
# those four keys unconditionally to arrows. Trade-off accepted: this
# keyboard can no longer type a literal comma/period/semicolon/slash; that's
# fine for flint/APPLaunch's launcher-style UI, which is the only thing this
# keyboard drives.
#
# Scancodes are USB HID usage-page-7 (keyboard/keypad) usage IDs, in the
# KEYBOARD_KEY_<hex> format systemd's hwdb builtin expects (see
# /usr/lib/udev/hwdb.d/60-keyboard.hwdb for more examples). 0x33/0x36/0x37
# are the plain Semicolon/Comma/Dot usages; 0x38 is plain Slash and 0x54 is
# the Keypad-Slash usage, since one firmware build sent 0x38 for Fn+/ and the
# other sent 0x54 for the same physical key — map both to be safe.
evdev:input:b0003v303Ap*
 KEYBOARD_KEY_70033=up
 KEYBOARD_KEY_70037=down
 KEYBOARD_KEY_70036=left
 KEYBOARD_KEY_70038=right
 KEYBOARD_KEY_70054=right
